   319	
   320	# Verify Acceptance Release
   321	
   322	## Goal
   323	
   324	Decide what the evidence actually proves without sharing the implementation role's authority or silently repairing the definition of done. Operate read-only by default.
   325	
   326	## Independence Boundary
   327	
   328	- Use a different session, context, or authorized human reviewer from the implementation role whenever practical.
   329	- Review the original request, every decision-scoped acceptance fragment in the requested release scope, its decision semantic key, accepted version, and invalidation keys, task-local `acceptance/index.md`, project-level `acceptance/index.md`, declared human `binding.md` and `checklist.md`, approval evidence, protected-test hashes, implementation diff identity, and raw test/runtime evidence.
   330	- Do not edit implementation or tests during review. If the user explicitly requests fixes, return findings to `$implement-acceptance-locked-change`, then rereview only the changed findings and affected evidence.
   331	- Reuse a prior review when contract, source diff, protected-test hashes, environment identity, and evidence identity are unchanged.
   332	
   333	## Decision-Scoped Verification And Local Reuse
   334	
   335	When an SSOT governs the change, validate the `.ssot` machine source and select review work from explicit decision, acceptance, implementation, evidence, and resource edges. Do not wait for an entire SSOT stage before reviewing a completed decision-scoped acceptance fragment. Fragment-level review may establish reusable evidence and findings, but it cannot declare the whole release `READY`.
   336	
   337	Bind every review lane to the fragment hash, decision semantic key, accepted version, and invalidation keys it evaluates. When an upstream decision or contract changes, reverify only evidence lanes whose invalidation keys match and any downstream synthesis that consumed them. Do not rerun every evidence lane or discard unrelated signed human results. Unchanged decision refs and evidence identities remain reusable.
   338	
   339	After all blocking fragments and shared convergence outputs in the declared release scope are accepted, one release-review owner makes one final serial release decision. Global completeness belongs only at that final synthesis boundary; it must not block earlier fragment review.
   340	
   341	## Maximum-Safe Parallel Verification
   342	
   343	Pin the selected fragment versions/hashes, decision refs, protected-test baselines, source diff, runtime identity, evidence identities, required roles, and one release-review owner serially. Then inventory all independent review surfaces and calculate:
   344	
   345	```text
   346	LOGICAL_LANE_TARGET = READY_INDEPENDENT_DELIVERABLES
   347	ACTUAL_CONCURRENCY = min(LOGICAL_LANE_TARGET, AVAILABLE_WRITABLE_L2_SLOTS)
   348	WAVE_COUNT = ceil(LOGICAL_LANE_TARGET / AVAILABLE_WRITABLE_L2_SLOTS)
   349	```
   350	
   351	If `AVAILABLE_WRITABLE_L2_SLOTS` is zero, mark dispatch `BLOCKED`; do not divide by zero, silently downgrade the sandbox, or serialize review through an unregistered executor.
   352	
   353	Use separate logical lanes for every independently reviewable protected-test partition, AC evidence partition, static/unit/integration/E2E/non-functional evidence set, implementation module or diff region, human-acceptance binding/run, external sandbox, exploratory result, production signal, and rollback control. These are categories, not a fixed lane count: split them further whenever inputs and conclusions are isolated. Reuse hash-identical accepted review results rather than launching duplicate lanes.
   354	
   355	Worker capacity changes waves only. Do not merge review lanes because they share a feature, layer, role, repository, deadline, reviewer, or output decision. Group only a proven `write-write`, `mutable-read-write`, `single-migration`, `single-generated-artifact`, or `inseparable-transaction` conflict, and record the exact resource-level reason. Read-only access to the same frozen source or evidence is parallel-safe.
   356	
   357	Review lanes have read-only authority even though their process receives a writable sandbox. They must not edit implementation, tests, the contract, human results, existing evidence, or SSOT status. Give each lane a unique return/evidence path, such as `acceptance/release/runs/<run-id>/evidence/review-lanes/<lane-id>.md`; only the release-review owner writes the final `result.md`, rebuilds indexes, reconciles AC/H dispositions, and makes one serial decision after all required lanes return. A lane may report a finding but may not repair it or independently declare the release `READY`.
   358	
   359	Use `$codex-explicit-worker-orchestration` for every dispatched review lane.
   360	Resolve and inspect the project-approved L2 wrapper first, then launch one bounded
   361	task per wrapper invocation:
   362	
   363	If the user or project adapter prioritizes an absolute `run-lw-luna.sh`, use it
   364	for eligible writable lanes and do not remap it to `run-l2.sh` or another model.
   365	
   366	```bash
   367	/absolute/path/run-l2.sh "<bounded acceptance-review lane packet>"
   368	```
   369	
   370	The inspected wrapper must end in `exec codex exec`, and its durable registration
   371	must preserve:
   372	
   373	```text
   374	codex exec -C /absolute/project/root --skip-git-repo-check --sandbox danger-full-access
   375	```
   376	
   377	Record `writable sandbox` on every lane. Do not use `.`, a relative root, `$PWD`,
   378	an unresolved variable, or an unrecorded wrapper invocation in place of this
   379	contract. Never use `spawn_agent`, a chat-internal subagent, or a same-process
   380	collaboration API as a substitute or fallback. Full filesystem capability does
   381	not authorize writes outside the unique lane-return path. Each packet must include
   382	task/node ID, direct parent, pinned identity tuple, exact read scope, the sole
   383	allowed return path, forbidden writes, selected requirements/evidence, commands,
   384	stop condition, and finding schema. The outer coordinator records command, PID,
   385	Codex session, log, return path, timestamps, and exit code for each process.
   386	
   387	When an SSOT governs release verification, express each ready review surface as a numbered sibling after only its own evidence-freeze predecessor. Add scoped synthesis nodes for evidence that must converge and one final serial release successor for the declared release scope. Do not hide all independent review work in one node, make it wait for unrelated decisions, or let parallel reviewers mutate `ACCEPTED` state.
   388	
   389	## Review Workflow
   390	
   391	1. Validate every selected acceptance fragment and confirm it was approved by the named authority before its implementation completed. Check current SSOT decision refs and invalidation keys, then dispatch the maximum conflict-free set of ready evidence-review lanes across fragments.
   392	2. Verify every protected acceptance test still exists and matches its approved hash. Treat deleted tests, weakened assertions, broad skips, unapproved snapshot updates, and fixture-specific production branches as blocking findings.
   393	3. Reconcile every `AC-*` item in each fragment with fresh or still-valid evidence. Confirm the test layer is strong enough for the claim; screenshots do not prove database side effects, and mocks do not prove a real external integration.
   394	4. Review implementation quality: module boundaries, reuse, transaction behavior, errors, dependencies, unrelated changes, hidden fallbacks, swallowed failures, hard-coding, and observability.
   395	5. Review the five machine layers proportionately: static, unit, integration/contract, critical E2E, and applicable non-functional checks.
   396	6. Verify real sandbox evidence for release-critical external services in addition to mock contracts. Record account/role, environment, timestamp, resource identity, and cleanup state.
   397	7. Resolve the project-level human workspace from the contract. Verify `binding.md` matches the exact task ID, SSOT path/node, contract version/hash, checklist hash, role, and blocking status. Read its stable checklist and the selected immutable `runs/<run-id>/result.md`; verify the run's contract, binding, and checklist snapshots and hashes before evaluating each signed `H-*` decision. An AI reviewer may prepare or observe the checklist but must not impersonate the product owner for blocking decisions.
   398	8. Check exploratory findings, rollback readiness, and production monitoring for high-risk changes. Define the metric, threshold, window, owner, and response action; “monitor logs” is not a release control.
   399	9. Create a new task-local `acceptance/release/runs/<run-id>/` review directory for each reviewed fragment or scoped synthesis. Bind the result to decision refs/invalidation keys, contract version/hash, source identity, runtime identity, and every accepted upstream run ID plus result hash. Never overwrite an earlier review or release decision.
   400	10. Rebuild and validate all affected task indexes and the project index. In an existing SSOT, reference selected machine, human, and release run IDs and hashes only from the affected nodes. Only each designated acceptance owner may move its node to `ACCEPTED`; only the release-review owner may issue the final release decision.
   401	11. Write findings first using [`references/release-review-template.md`](references/release-review-template.md). Do not use test counts or code coverage as a completion claim.
   402	
   403	## Decision States
   404	
   405	- `READY`: all blocking ACs have adequate evidence, protected tests are intact, required human items are explicitly accepted, and release controls are ready.
   406	- `CONDITIONAL`: machine evidence passes, but named non-blocking follow-up or time-bounded production evidence remains. State the exact condition and owner.
   407	- `NOT READY`: any blocking AC, protected-test integrity check, required human acceptance, rollback control, or environment evidence is missing or failed.
   408	- `BLOCKED`: the review cannot obtain required credentials, environment, device, approval, or authoritative input. Do not convert lack of evidence into a pass.
   409	
   410	## Output
   411	
   412	Report severity-ordered findings with file/evidence references, the AC/H traceability disposition, protected-test integrity, machine-layer results, human acceptance status, external sandbox evidence, monitoring and rollback readiness, final decision, and residual risk.
